ot on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat is one of 2026's most competitive races. With Gary Peters retiring, Democrat Abdul El-Sayed faces Republican Mike Rogers in a state with an even partisan baseline. Recent polls from mid-to-late September consistently show El-Sayed leading by roughly three to five points, including strong results from high-quality pollsters, and prediction markets give him about a 70% chance of winning.\n\nThe race remains genuinely contested. Expert raters are split between Tossup and Lean D, and outside groups have spent heavily against El-Sayed, who has also drawn attacks over his progressive profile. Rogers, a former congressman, has struggled to gain traction despite the favorable spending environment. The forecast gives El-Sayed a modest edge consistent with the polling average, with meaningful uncertainty typical of a tossup-adjacent open seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7553, "margin": 3.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7118, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7565, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7778, "margin": 3.5}]}